# config/gta9_guard.env copied byte for byte, with ENABLE_SUSFS flipped to true
# and nothing else touched. Same rule as the copy of gta9_7patch.env that the
# KCFLAGS incident established: copy, never retype. The two files were compared
# and differ on exactly one line.
#
# What this enables, and what it does not.
#
# It enables the kernel side. SUSFS is two halves that meet at a shared
# contract, and only one of them was missing:
#
#   kernel side   fs/susfs.c, include/linux/susfs.h, include/linux/susfs_def.h
#                 plus hooks into 24 kernel files. This was absent.
#   driver side   ReSukiSU already has all of it. kernel/Kconfig:104 declares
#                 config KSU_SUSFS, kernel/supercall/dispatch.c:995 implements
#                 ksu_handle_susfs_cmd, and its switch handles
#                 CMD_SUSFS_SHOW_VERSION, CMD_SUSFS_SHOW_VARIANT and
#                 CMD_SUSFS_SHOW_ENABLED_FEATURES -- the three commands ksud
#                 sends as 0x555e1, 0x555e2 and 0x555e3.
#
# That is why ksud answered "Unsupported SuSFS command" while this kernel ran:
# the dispatch that would answer them was already in the driver, and the
# handler behind it was compiled out because CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS was never set.
#
# Because the driver already integrates SUSFS, susfs_apply() in scripts/patches.sh
# finds susfs_is_bundled() true, logs that it is skipping
# 10_enable_susfs_for_ksu.patch, and does not try to apply a patch that was
# written for SukiSU-Ultra's driver and could not fit this one anyway. That is
# the correct path and it is already wired up; no change to patches.sh needed.
#
# Measured, not assumed: patch -p1 --force --fuzz=3 of the gki-android12-5.10
# kernel patch against this tree puts 70 hunks in, one of them with fuzz 3,
# zero rejects, all 24 files changed. The single fuzzed hunk is the include
# block in fs/namespace.c and it lands correctly. See docs/SUSFS_PORT.md.
#
# The one thing this cannot tell us is whether the result boots. A clean patch
# and a clean compile are not a working kernel, and only the tablet can answer
# that.
#
# A copy of config/gta9_7patch.env, byte for byte, with one value appended to
# EXTRA_DEFCONFIG. It is a copy rather than an edited-in-place file for the
# reason gta9_7patch.env is one: KCFLAGS was once retyped by hand and cost
# thirty-three minutes before a warning turned out to be an error because of it.
# Every other line here is identical to that file, including KCFLAGS.
#
# What this fixes, and how it was found.
#
# Two kernels built from this tree booted the tablet into recovery instead of
# into Android. The twenty-nine-patch build and the seven-patch build failed
# alike, with clean links and byte-identical ramdisks. What the tablet's own
# firmware finally settled was CONFIG_MODVERSIONS=y: 202 stock Samsung modules
# record a CRC for each symbol they import, insmod refuses a module whose CRCs
# disagree with the running kernel, and comparing all 2,305 of them against
# Module.symvers from run 36788148559 left exactly one symbol missing:
#
#   __stack_chk_guard   demanded by 155 of the 202 modules, CRC 0x8f678b07
#                      absent from Module.symvers, and 0 occurrences of the
#                      string in the kernel image itself
#
# It is absent because of this, in arch/arm64/kernel/process.c:
#
#   #if defined(CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR) && !defined(CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK)
#   unsigned long __stack_chk_guard __ro_after_init;
#   EXPORT_SYMBOL(__stack_chk_guard);
#   #endif
#
# The definition is there and so is the export; the whole block is conditioned on
# CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK being unset. Samsung's kernel has no such option
# at all -- it is absent from the config read off the tablet -- so the block is
# compiled and 155 modules find their canary. This tree does have the option,
# kconfig turns on its default, the block is skipped, and every one of those
# 155 modules is refused. Among them are cqhci (UFS storage), cfg80211 and
# clk-common, so init has no storage and the kernel dies where nothing can log it.
#
# Turning it off restores the behaviour the tablet's modules were compiled
# against. The prediction is falsifiable: the next build's Module.symvers must
# contain __stack_chk_guard with CRC 0x8f678b07, and scripts/crccheck.py compare
# must report zero missing symbols.
#
# Five symbols still disagree on CRC and are NOT addressed here, because they are
# not what stops the boot: sync_file_create (mtk_sync.ko) and the four cpufreq
# entry points (blocktag, mtk_pbm, musb_boost, teeperf, mediatek-cpufreq-hw).
# Those five are prototype drift between this newer tree and Samsung's older one,
# which is the same class of problem and worth the same treatment afterwards.
#
# One more difference worth knowing about, visible in the same diff but left
# alone: the tablet's kernel was built with clang 12.0.5 and this tree is built
# with 16.0.6. That is the largest single difference between the two builds and
# it is not addressed here either, because there is no measurement yet that
# points at it.

KERNEL_SOURCE=https://github.com/vickcoy31-ux/kernelvalidevo
KERNEL_SOURCE_BRANCH=14.0
KERNEL_CONFIG=/config/gta9_stock_defconfig
KERNEL_NAME=gta9

# The tablet's own `uname -a`. This is the part that has to match for the
# stock modules in /vendor_dlkm to load, so it stays pinned here too.
KERNEL_LOCALVERSION=-android12-9-28698995

PATCH_WHITELIST=1,3,4,22,27,28,29

KSU_VARIANT=resukisu
KSU_HOOK_MODE=tracepoint
ENABLE_SUSFS=true
ENABLE_PATH_UMOUNT=false
ENABLE_HIDE_STUFF=false
ENABLE_KPM=false

USE_CUSTOM_CLANG=true
CUSTOM_CLANG_SOURCE=https://github.com/ZyCromerZ/Clang/releases/download/16.0.6-20260807-release/Clang-16.0.6-20260807.tar.gz
USE_LLVM=true
ENABLE_GCC_ARM64=false
ENABLE_GCC_ARM32=false

DISABLE_CC_WERROR=true
DISABLE_LTO=false
ADD_KPROBES_CONFIG=false
ADD_OVERLAYFS_CONFIG=false
ENABLE_CCACHE=true
REMOVE_UNUSED_PACKAGES=true
NEED_DTBO=false
KERNEL_IMAGE_NAME=Image.gz
KCFLAGS=-Wno-error -Wno-error=deprecated-non-prototype -Wno-error=unused-but-set-variable -Wno-error=unused-function -Wno-error=implicit-int -Wno-error=frame-larger-than -Wno-error=comment -Wno-error=implicit-function-declaration -Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types -Wno-error=unused-result -Wno-error=declaration-after-statement

# No Odin image from this run. It would have to go through a .tar to be flashed
# anyway, and the last one was assembled by hand and tested; the workflow's
# repack writes 4 bytes of AVB footer that Odin then rejects. The kernel image
# is the deliverable, and the boot image is made on the machine that has the
# stock image.
BUILD_BOOT_IMG=false
BUILD_ODIN_BOOT_IMG=false
SOURCE_BOOT_IMAGE=boot/boot.img
USE_CUSTOM_ANYKERNEL3=false
CUSTOM_ANYKERNEL3_SOURCE=
CUSTOM_ANYKERNEL3_BRANCH=
EXTRA_CMDS=
CUSTOM_CMDS=

# Only the twenty-four MT6983 options, which name a different chipset and do not
# compile against this branch's sound driver and clock tree. Everything else
# stays as the device has it. TRIM_UNUSED_KSYMS is off because its whitelist
# was never published; the stock config has it on, and leaving it off only means
# this kernel exports more symbols than the tablet's modules need.
#
# CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK=n is the one that matters, and it is not in the
# tablet's config at all. This tree has the option and kconfig defaults it to y,
# which skips the block that defines and exports __stack_chk_guard -- a symbol
# 155 of the tablet's 202 modules import. With it off, the block is compiled
# again and those modules find the canary they were built against.
# CONFIG_MTK_COMBO, the WiFi and Bluetooth combo chip, is never pinned.
EXTRA_DEFCONFIG=CONFIG_STACKPROTECTOR_PER_TASK=n CONFIG_TRIM_UNUSED_KSYMS=n CONFIG_SND_SOC_MT6983=n CONFIG_SND_SOC_MT6983_MT6338=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_ADSP=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_CAM=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_CCU=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_IMG=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_MMSYS=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_IMP_IIC_WRAP=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_MDPSYS=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_MFG=n CONFIG_COMMON_CLK_MT6983_VCODEC=n CONFIG_DEVAPC_MT6983=n CONFIG_MTK_SLBC_MT6983=n CONFIG_MTK_CM_MGR_MT6983=n CONFIG_MTK_DCM_MT6983=n CONFIG_MTK_LPM_MT6983=n CONFIG_MTK_SWPM_MT6983=n CONFIG_MTK_MDP_MT6983=n CONFIG_TOUCHSCREEN_MT6983_ALPHA_SUPPORT=n CONFIG_PINCTRL_MT6983=n CONFIG_GAUGE_MT6983_ALPHA_SUPPORT=n CONFIG_REGULATOR_MT6983_ALPHA_SUPPORT=n CONFIG_MTK_GPU_MT6983_SUPPORT=n